#!/usr/bin/env python3.11
"""co-rollover — hand this tmux session to a successor (ADR-0069 Slice 5).

Replaces the `/rollover` skill's inline bash. The skill still writes
`handoff.md` (unchanged) and still tells the operator what happened; this script owns
everything from "spawn the successor" through "the predecessor is fenced and
will close once the successor confirms pickup."

Two invocations, run from two different Claude Code sessions:

  co-rollover [--product NAME]
      Run by the PREDECESSOR, right after it has written handoff.md. Spawns
      the successor in a new window of the SAME tmux session, verifies it
      came up, `select-window`s to it, and sets this session's rollover state
      to `rolling` — which `tools/rollover-trigger.py`'s PreToolUse fence then
      denies every further tool call in, unconditionally.

  co-rollover --picked-up <handoff-path>
      Run by the SUCCESSOR, once `/handoff-pickup` has read the handoff at
      that exact path. Forwards, SYNCHRONOUSLY, any message that landed in
      the predecessor's held queue after the handoff was written (Slice 7,
      epic issue_01M1S2X8ENKYEGSDE3H0REFD3G — `co_rollover.
      forward_late_messages`), THEN records pickup and starts a DETACHED
      close-out (kill the predecessor's pane, then renumber so the
      successor becomes window 1) — detached so this command returns
      immediately rather than blocking the successor's own turn on it. The
      close-out never fires without a recorded pickup: that ordering is
      what keeps a failed spawn from stranding the operator with zero
      sessions (the skill's original safety rule, carried forward rather
      than re-derived).

      Forwarding runs BEFORE the side channel's `picked_up` gate flips
      (round 2, PR #588), on purpose: the gate is what a retried
      `--picked-up` reads to skip a second close-out, and if it also gated
      forwarding, a crash between the two would leave `picked_up` true with
      the queue never read — a held message dropped with no record it ever
      existed, forever, since nothing after that point ever calls
      `forward_late_messages` again. Reading the not-yet-flipped side
      channel instead means a crash-then-retry re-forwards the queue
      exactly as it stood — a possible duplicate of a message an earlier,
      interrupted call already delivered. A visible duplicate beats a
      silent loss. The close-out gate itself is untouched: it still flips
      exactly once, so a retry — crash-driven or a plain duplicate
      dispatch — never starts a second one.

THE HANDOFF DIRECTORY comes from `agentctl session-dir --product <product>`,
called by THIS process. With `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` set, that returns the
directory the session recorded on its first call, so this script reads the same
`handoff.md` the handoff was written to. The skill's step 1 also exports
`CONVIVY_SESSION_DIR` to that directory, which `session-dir` returns unchanged
(see `bin/agentctl`'s `cmd_session_dir`). With neither variable set, every call
mints a fresh, numbered directory.

FAILS LOUD, NOT OPEN — unlike `tools/rollover-trigger.py`. That hook fires on
every tool call in every session and a stuck failure there wedges the
machine; this is a foreground command run once, by intent, and its caller
(the model, following the rollover skill) needs to know plainly whether the
handoff actually happened. A failed spawn prints the successor pane's last
lines and exits non-zero WITHOUT marking anything `rolling` — the predecessor
stays live and unfenced, for the model to report and keep working in (the
skill's "never strand the operator" rule, Slice 6's territory for anything
past reporting it).

ITS `.claude/` MAY NOT BE UNTOUCHED, THOUGH. When a predecessor pid was found
to exempt, the successor's forced re-individuate starts running inside the
detached window the instant `spawn_successor` creates it — well before
`verify_up` can detect a failure. A spawn that fails after that rewrite has
started can report failure with the predecessor's own `.claude/agents/` and
`.claude/skills/` already replaced by the successor's profile
(`co_rollover.spawn_successor`'s docstring has the full timing and the
unguarded gap this leaves).

A FAILURE MUST LEAN TOWARD "UNFENCED", NEVER "STUCK ROLLING WITH NO WAY BACK".
`_cmd_spawn` writes the side channel BEFORE calling `mark_rolling`, on
purpose: those are the only two steps left once the successor is verified
up, and only the side-channel write can raise (a removed handoff directory,
a full disk). Marking rolling first and writing the side channel second
would let that failure leave the predecessor permanently fenced with no
side channel for any successor to ever find — the fence has no built-in way
out (see `rollover_policy.mark_rolling`'s docstring; `session-agent rollover
<name> clear` is the operator's manual escape hatch).

A FAILED SPAWN PARKS RATHER THAN JUST REPORTING (Slice 6, ADR-0069/epic
comment 2402 ruling (b)). There is no automatic retry: `spawn_successor`
raising `CoRolloverError`, or `verify_up` timing out or matching a known
failure signature, both now log one alert signal for the operator
and write a `parked` rollover state before returning non-zero — see
`_park_and_file`, and `rollover_policy.mark_parked`'s own docstring for why
`parked`, unlike `rolling`, is not a fence: the predecessor stays fully
usable while it waits for the operator.

THE CLOSE-OUT VERIFIES THE PANE IT IS ABOUT TO KILL. `mark_rolling` records
the predecessor pane's own PID and the tmux server's `#{session_id}`
alongside its `pane_id` — tmux reassigns both pane ids and session ids from
zero on every server restart, so a `pane_id` recorded before a restart can
otherwise name a live pane in an unrelated session by the time
`--picked-up` runs (2026-09-13, %3 after the 15:46 MST restart named live
panes elsewhere). `co_rollover.close_out` checks the live pane's identity
against what was recorded before it ever calls `kill-pane`.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import os
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path

_SELF = Path(__file__).resolve()
_LIB_DIR = _SELF.parent.parent / "lib" / "agent-platform"
sys.path.insert(0, str(_LIB_DIR))
import co_rollover as CR  # noqa: E402
import signal_client as SC  # noqa: E402
import rollover_policy as RP  # noqa: E402
from private_crew import private_home_for  # noqa: E402

# Resolved beside THIS file (ARCH-229), not `~/.local/bin/agentctl` — a
# worktree's own copy of this script must call ITS OWN worktree's agentctl,
# not whatever primary has checked out.
_AGENTCTL = _SELF.parent / "agentctl"


def _handoff_dir(product: str) -> Path:
    try:
        done = subprocess.run([str(_AGENTCTL), "session-dir", "--product", product],
                              capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=15)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as exc:
        print(f"co-rollover: could not run agentctl session-dir: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(1)
    if done.returncode != 0 or not done.stdout.strip():
        print(f"co-rollover: agentctl session-dir failed: {done.stderr.strip()}", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(1)
    return Path(done.stdout.strip())


def _require_session_id() -> str:
    session_id = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID")
    if not session_id:
        print("co-rollover: CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID is unset — this must run inside a co session",
              file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(1)
    return session_id


# --- Slice 6: a failed spawn parks and logs one operator signal ------------
#
# `reason` values `mark_parked` records and `_park_and_file`'s signal title
# names — one per failure branch in `_cmd_spawn`, so an operator reading the
# state file or the signal can tell which without reading this script.
_REASON_SPAWN_ERROR = "spawn_error"
_REASON_VERIFY_UP_FAILED = "verify_up_failed"


# A park is written whatever the signal returns, and it runs on the rollover path, so the signal
# gets a 5s timeout per socket operation rather than the delivery budget (30s per attempt).
# post_structured makes up to three attempts with backoff, so a store that stops answering costs
# about 19.5 seconds. A store that trickles bytes can exceed that, since urllib applies the timeout
# to each read.
_PARK_SIGNAL_TIMEOUT = 5
# `medium`, matching the spawn-failure park's severity on its item: a parked session is recoverable
# and stays fully usable, so the operator needs to know, but nothing is getting worse.
_PARK_SEVERITY = "medium"


def _park_and_file(predecessor_session_id: str, *, session_name: str,
                   predecessor_pane_id: str, handoff_path: Path, product: str,
                   cwd: str, reason: str, detail: str | None, pane_text: str | None,
                   successor_window: int | None,
                   successor_pane: str | None) -> tuple[str | None, str]:
    """Log one alert signal for the operator, then write the `parked` rollover state
— always, regardless of whether logging succeeded. Returns the signal status:
`created`, `duplicate`, `skip` (no token), `black-box` or an `error:`-prefixed string.
A session inside a private crew logs no signal (ADR-0043); the state records `black-box`.

THE STATUS IS RETURNED AND RECORDED BECAUSE IT IS THE ONLY RECORD OF WHY LOGGING
FAILED. `post_alert` never raises, so a wire error comes back as `error:<code>`
and a missing token as `skip`. Taking only a yes or no here would discard the
reason at the one point anything held it, and a failed log and a clean park would
leave identical evidence.

LOGGING RUNS FIRST so `mark_parked` records the status in its one state write.
`post_alert` never raises, so the park always still runs. "A parked session with
no signal is recoverable; a lost park is not" (the build's own words).

`dedup_key` is the predecessor's session id plus the handoff path (not just its
filename, which is always literally `handoff.md` — `_handoff_dir`'s directory is
what makes the path unique per rollover attempt): stable across a retried
`co-rollover` against the SAME predecessor session and the SAME unmodified
handoff, so a retry that fails again answers `duplicate` instead of logging twice.
"""
    predecessor_window = CR.pane_window_index(predecessor_pane_id)
    lines = [
        "An automatic rollover's successor spawn failed; there is no "
        "automatic retry, and the predecessor has parked.",
        "",
        f"- failure path: {reason}",
        f"- predecessor session id: {predecessor_session_id}",
        f"- tmux session: {session_name}"
        + (f", window {predecessor_window}" if predecessor_window is not None else ""),
        f"- predecessor pane: {predecessor_pane_id}",
        f"- handoff: {handoff_path}",
    ]
    if detail:
        lines.append(f"- error: {detail}")
    if successor_window is not None:
        lines.append(
            f"- successor window: {successor_window}"
            + (f" (pane {successor_pane})" if successor_pane else ""))
    if pane_text:
        lines += ["", "Last lines of the successor's pane:", "```"]
        lines += pane_text.splitlines()[-30:]
        lines.append("```")
    lines += [
        "",
        "The predecessor session stays live and usable — nothing is fenced "
        "— but it will not seek or roll over again on its own. Once this is "
        "resolved, run `session-agent rollover <name> clear` to un-park it.",
    ]

    # ADR-0043: the gate is before any of the session's text is sent. `lines` quotes the handoff and
    # the successor's pane, and `cwd` is the only thing that places the session in a crew.
    # `private_home_for` answers UNKNOWN when it cannot tell, and UNKNOWN is not None, so it fails
    # closed.
    if private_home_for(cwd) is not None:
        status = "black-box"
    else:
        status = SC.post_alert(
            "co-rollover-park",
            SC.sanitise_body(
                f"co-rollover: successor spawn failed ({reason}), session parked\n\n"
                + "\n".join(lines)),
            product=product,
            dedup_key=f"co-rollover-park:{predecessor_session_id}:{handoff_path}",
            timeout=_PARK_SIGNAL_TIMEOUT, meta={"severity": _PARK_SEVERITY})

    RP.mark_parked(predecessor_session_id, reason=reason, handoff_path=str(handoff_path),
                   issue_id=None, issue_status=status, issue_product=product)
    return status


def _report_parked(issue_status: str = "unknown") -> None:
    """Name the signal status rather than pointing at the scrollback. A failed log
    prints its status here, so a park whose signal did not land reads as incomplete
    instead of as complete."""
    if issue_status in ("created", "duplicate"):
        print(f"co-rollover: logged a signal for the operator ({issue_status}); "
              "this session has parked.", file=sys.stderr)
    elif issue_status == "black-box":
        print("co-rollover: this session is inside a black-box crew, which logs no signal "
              "(ADR-0043); this session has parked.", file=sys.stderr)
    else:
        print(f"co-rollover: could not log a signal for this failure "
              f"(logging returned {issue_status}); this session has parked anyway "
              "— tell the operator directly.", file=sys.stderr)


def _cmd_spawn(args) -> int:
    predecessor_session_id = _require_session_id()

    try:
        session_name, predecessor_pane_id = CR.current_session_and_pane()
    except CR.CoRolloverError as exc:
        print(f"co-rollover: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    # Captured now, not at close-out time: `close_out` verifies this against
    # what tmux reports for `predecessor_pane_id` right before it kills
    # anything, since a tmux server restart reassigns both pane ids and
    # session ids from zero and can otherwise make an old `pane_id` name an
    # unrelated live pane (2026-09-13, %3 after the 15:46 MST restart).
    predecessor_identity = CR.pane_identity(predecessor_pane_id)
    if predecessor_identity is None:
        print(f"co-rollover: could not resolve this pane's own identity "
              f"({predecessor_pane_id!r}) — refusing to roll over", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    cwd = CR.git_toplevel()
    product = args.product or os.path.basename(cwd)

    handoff_dir = _handoff_dir(product)
    handoff_path = handoff_dir / "handoff.md"
    if not handoff_path.is_file():
        print(f"co-rollover: no handoff.md at {handoff_path} — write it (as /handoff-leave "
              "does) before running co-rollover", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    # The predecessor's own `claude` pids, if any can be found beneath its
    # pane (issue_01M2FDBJS8GMA16DX4XNBVH2K6) — passed to `spawn_successor` so
    # the successor's forced re-individuate can exempt exactly these pids
    # from knowledge-ctx's live-process guard instead of skipping the
    # re-individuate outright. Every pid at the pane, not just one:
    # `tmux_transcript.claude_pids_under` can legitimately return more than
    # one (its own docstring's 2026-07-22 computer-use-fork case), and
    # exempting only the first would leave a second, equally-legitimate pid
    # tripping the guard. Empty when no claude process resolves (the pane's
    # shell already exited, or the tree is deeper than
    # `claude_pids_for_pane`'s bounded walk) — `spawn_successor` falls back to
    # today's skip in that case, exactly as before this pid lookup existed.
    predecessor_claude_pids = CR.predecessor_claude_pids(predecessor_identity["pane_pid"])

    # ADR-0069, the operator's 2026-09-16 ruling: the successor keeps the predecessor's
    # permission mode. `rollover_policy.evaluate` (fired from `Stop`/
    # `UserPromptSubmit`) already tracks this field, but that reading can be
    # stale by the time a rollover actually runs — a Shift+Tab mode change
    # fires no hook at all. `tools/rollover-trigger.py`'s `PreToolUse` branch
    # records the CURRENT mode on every firing that isn't itself the `rolling`
    # fence, and the PreToolUse firing for THIS invocation (the Bash tool call
    # running `co-rollover` itself) is what makes this read fresh as of right
    # now rather than as of the predecessor's last Stop/UserPromptSubmit.
    predecessor_permission_mode = RP.read_state(predecessor_session_id).get("permission_mode")

    try:
        spawned = CR.spawn_successor(session_name, cwd, product, str(handoff_path),
                                      exempt_pids=predecessor_claude_pids,
                                      permission_mode=predecessor_permission_mode)
    except CR.CoRolloverError as exc:
        print(f"co-rollover: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        issue_status = _park_and_file(
            predecessor_session_id, session_name=session_name,
            predecessor_pane_id=predecessor_pane_id, handoff_path=handoff_path,
            product=product, cwd=cwd, reason=_REASON_SPAWN_ERROR, detail=str(exc),
            pane_text=None, successor_window=None, successor_pane=None)
        _report_parked(issue_status)
        return 1

    ok, pane_text = CR.verify_up(session_name, spawned["window_index"])
    if not ok:
        print("co-rollover: the successor did not come up. Do NOT switch — this session is "
              "unfenced and live, but if a predecessor pid was exempted its .claude/ profile "
              "may already have been rewritten by the failed successor's re-individuate. "
              "Last lines of its pane:", file=sys.stderr)
        for line in pane_text.splitlines()[-30:]:
            print(f"    {line}", file=sys.stderr)
        issue_status = _park_and_file(
            predecessor_session_id, session_name=session_name,
            predecessor_pane_id=predecessor_pane_id, handoff_path=handoff_path,
            product=product, cwd=cwd, reason=_REASON_VERIFY_UP_FAILED, detail=None,
            pane_text=pane_text, successor_window=spawned["window_index"],
            successor_pane=spawned["pane_id"])
        _report_parked(issue_status)
        return 1

    CR.select_window(session_name, spawned["window_index"])

    # Turn `remain-on-exit` back off now that the successor is confirmed up
    # (`spawn_successor` turned it on only so a failed spawn stayed
    # diagnosable — see its own comment and `clear_remain_on_exit`'s
    # docstring). Best-effort: a failure here must not fail the rollover,
    # since the successor is already live and selected by this point; it only
    # means this window won't auto-close when its session later ends.
    if not CR.clear_remain_on_exit(session_name, spawned["window_index"]):
        print(f"co-rollover: could not turn off remain-on-exit on window "
              f"{spawned['window_index']} — it will not auto-close once this "
              "session ends", file=sys.stderr)

    predecessor = {"session_id": predecessor_session_id, "pane_id": predecessor_pane_id,
                   "pane_pid": predecessor_identity["pane_pid"],
                   "tmux_session_id": predecessor_identity["tmux_session_id"]}
    successor = {"session_id": None, "pane_id": spawned["pane_id"]}
    # The side channel is written BEFORE `mark_rolling`: a failure in either
    # step must leave the session unfenced (never rolling with no side
    # channel for a successor to ever find), so whichever one can fail is the
    # one that must run first. `write_side_channel` can raise (no handoff
    # directory, a full disk); `mark_rolling` cannot (`_atomic_write` swallows
    # its own OSError) — see each function's own docstring.
    CR.write_side_channel(handoff_path, {
        "tmux_session": session_name,
        "predecessor": predecessor,
        "successor": successor,
        "successor_window": spawned["window_index"],
        "picked_up": False,
        "picked_up_at": None,
    })
    RP.mark_rolling(predecessor_session_id, predecessor=predecessor, successor=successor,
                    successor_window=spawned["window_index"])

    print(f"co-rollover: successor is up in window {spawned['window_index']} "
          f"(pane {spawned['pane_id']}); handoff at {handoff_path}. "
          "This session has rolled over — end your turn.")
    return 0


def _cmd_picked_up(args) -> int:
    successor_session_id = _require_session_id()
    handoff_path = Path(args.picked_up)

    data = CR.read_side_channel(handoff_path)
    if data is None:
        print(f"co-rollover: no co-rollover state found beside {handoff_path!r}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    if data.get("picked_up"):
        # A retried `--picked-up` (the successor re-running it, or a
        # duplicate dispatch) that reached an earlier, completed call's
        # gate flip: that call already forwarded any late message this
        # handoff gets and already started the one close-out it gets. Do
        # neither again.
        print("co-rollover: pickup already recorded; not starting another close-out.")
        return 0

    predecessor = data.get("predecessor") or {}
    predecessor_pane_id = predecessor.get("pane_id")
    predecessor_session_id = predecessor.get("session_id")
    successor_pane_id = (data.get("successor") or {}).get("pane_id")
    tmux_session = data.get("tmux_session")

    # Slice 7 (epic issue_01M1S2X8ENKYEGSDE3H0REFD3G): forward anything that
    # landed in the predecessor's held queue AFTER the handoff was written —
    # SYNCHRONOUS, not part of the detached close-out below, because the
    # close-out's own stdout/stderr are `DEVNULL` (see the `Popen` call
    # further down) and a forwarding failure must reach somewhere an
    # operator or this turn's own transcript can see it. Never wedges the
    # pickup itself: `forward_late_messages` never raises, it returns
    # per-message failures instead.
    #
    # Deliberately reads `picked_up` above rather than going through
    # `mark_picked_up` first (round 2, PR #588) — see the module docstring
    # for why forwarding must run before that gate flips, and which failure
    # (a possible duplicate, never a silent loss) this ordering accepts.
    if predecessor_session_id and successor_pane_id and tmux_session:
        sent, failures = CR.forward_late_messages(
            handoff_path, predecessor_session_id, successor_pane_id, tmux_session)
        if sent:
            print(f"co-rollover: forwarded {sent} held message(s) from the predecessor.")
        for failure in failures:
            print(f"co-rollover: {failure}", file=sys.stderr)
    else:
        missing = [name for name, val in (
            ("predecessor session id", predecessor_session_id),
            ("successor pane id", successor_pane_id),
            ("tmux session", tmux_session),
        ) if not val]
        print(f"co-rollover: skipping message forwarding — missing {', '.join(missing)} on "
              "record", file=sys.stderr)
        CR.log_forwarding_skipped(missing)

    try:
        picked_up = CR.mark_picked_up(handoff_path, successor_session_id)
    except CR.CoRolloverError as exc:
        print(f"co-rollover: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    if not picked_up.get("picked_up_this_call"):
        # A concurrent `--picked-up` call for the same handoff reached the
        # gate first: the forwarding pass above may have just re-sent
        # messages that call is about to deliver too (the same accepted
        # duplicate-over-loss trade the crash-retry case takes), but only
        # ONE call ever starts the close-out.
        print("co-rollover: pickup already recorded; not starting another close-out.")
        return 0

    if predecessor_pane_id and tmux_session:
        # DETACHED: the close-out (kill the predecessor's pane, then
        # renumber) runs independently so this command returns immediately
        # rather than the successor's own turn blocking on it. The
        # predecessor's own pane_pid/tmux_session_id (recorded at
        # `mark_rolling` time) travel along so `close_out` can verify the
        # named pane is still the one it was told to close before touching
        # it — see `co_rollover.close_out`'s docstring.
        subprocess.Popen(
            [sys.executable, str(_SELF), "--close-out", tmux_session, predecessor_pane_id,
             str(predecessor.get("pane_pid")), str(predecessor.get("tmux_session_id"))],
            start_new_session=True, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
        )
        print("co-rollover: pickup recorded; closing out the predecessor.")
    else:
        print("co-rollover: pickup recorded (no predecessor pane on record — nothing to close out).")
    return 0


def _cmd_close_out(args) -> int:
    session_name, predecessor_pane_id, pane_pid, tmux_session_id = args.close_out
    CR.close_out(session_name, predecessor_pane_id,
                expected_pane_pid=pane_pid, expected_tmux_session_id=tmux_session_id)
    return 0


def main() -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])
    ap.add_argument("--product", default=None,
                    help="override the product slug (default: basename of the git toplevel)")
    ap.add_argument("--picked-up", metavar="HANDOFF_PATH", default=None,
                    help="record pickup for the handoff at HANDOFF_PATH, then close out "
                         "the predecessor")
    ap.add_argument("--close-out", nargs=4,
                    metavar=("TMUX_SESSION", "PANE_ID", "PANE_PID", "TMUX_SESSION_ID"),
                    default=None,
                    help=argparse.SUPPRESS)  # internal: the detached close-out step
    args = ap.parse_args()

    if args.close_out:
        return _cmd_close_out(args)
    if args.picked_up:
        return _cmd_picked_up(args)
    return _cmd_spawn(args)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
